WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:03.634
-Also, we've had
an especially great week

00:00:03.634 --> 00:00:06.033
because we've had Fred Armisen
back with us

00:00:06.033 --> 00:00:08.801
leading the band on drums.

00:00:08.801 --> 00:00:11.767
And Fred, every time I get
to spend a week with you,

00:00:11.767 --> 00:00:13.234
the most delightful part
is hearing

00:00:13.234 --> 00:00:16.501
about all your other projects,
the amazing things you're doing.

00:00:16.501 --> 00:00:17.901
You just really are...

00:00:17.901 --> 00:00:20.400
You are the multi-est
of multi-hyphenates.

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.567
-Thank you.

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:24.167
-And... this is obviously
the time of year

00:00:24.167 --> 00:00:25.767
a lot of people
are going apple picking.

00:00:25.767 --> 00:00:28.100
I heard that you just
bought an apple orchard,

00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:31.701
but you're not going to use it
in the conventional way.

00:00:31.701 --> 00:00:35.334
-No, I'm going to fill it more
with books,

00:00:35.334 --> 00:00:39.267
because there's something
very not intellectual

00:00:39.267 --> 00:00:40.901
about apple picking.

00:00:40.901 --> 00:00:45.300
So I thought, well, how can we
make this a more brainy affair?

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:47.400
How can we do it
so that people discuss books

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.434
and talk about literature?

00:00:49.434 --> 00:00:52.400
So, we don't have
the shelves quite yet,

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:53.901
but we've got, like,
piles of books.

00:00:53.901 --> 00:00:57.400
So you go in there, and instead
of apple picking, pick a book.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.567
[ Laughter ]

00:00:58.567 --> 00:01:00.067
-So there are --
let me just clarify --

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:02.367
there are actual apple trees
with apples,

00:01:02.367 --> 00:01:06.467
but you want people to come
and just go get a book instead?

00:01:06.467 --> 00:01:08.133
-Yeah. So it's sort
of like, "Okay, great.

00:01:08.133 --> 00:01:11.067
These are apples. Fantastic.
Let's talk about these books."

00:01:11.067 --> 00:01:12.567
-So wait, I guess
my question would be,

00:01:12.567 --> 00:01:14.501
it seems like you wanted
to open a bookstore,

00:01:14.501 --> 00:01:16.701
and why wouldn't you
just do that?

00:01:16.701 --> 00:01:18.501
-I thought the same thing.

00:01:18.501 --> 00:01:20.901
[ Laughter ]

00:01:20.901 --> 00:01:22.067
-Uh-huh.

00:01:22.067 --> 00:01:24.000
[ Laughter ]

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:25.901
-Like, why wouldn't I want
to just do that?

00:01:25.901 --> 00:01:29.634
But then, I had the orchard,
and these people showed up,

00:01:29.634 --> 00:01:31.434
and they, you know,
they bought tickets,

00:01:31.434 --> 00:01:34.601
and I was like, "Look, let's --
let's talk instead.

00:01:34.601 --> 00:01:36.000
Let's -- Let's read."

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:38.067
-Is it against --
Is it against the rules

00:01:38.067 --> 00:01:41.801
if they want to get an apple?
-Yes. It's very --

00:01:41.801 --> 00:01:43.868
So I want to tell everyone
it's very against the rules

00:01:43.868 --> 00:01:45.734
if you want to get an apple.

00:01:45.734 --> 00:01:48.267
-[ Laughs ]
What's it called?

00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:50.300
-We don't have a name for it.
-Okay.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:52.133
Why would you?
I feel like it's --

00:01:52.133 --> 00:01:53.567
I mean, basically,
it sells itself.

00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:55.100
-Thank you.
-You're welcome.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:57.334
-Thanks.
-Can I come?

00:01:57.334 --> 00:01:59.968
-Please. Yes.
-Can I --

00:01:59.968 --> 00:02:03.267
If I'm very hungry,
hypothetically...

00:02:04.968 --> 00:02:06.634
I'm not --

00:02:06.634 --> 00:02:08.467
I'm -- I'm not -- I'm not even
going to ask. [ Laughs ]

